   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the labor law in relation to removing certain provisions\nrelating to covered airport workers; and in relation to minimum wage rates for\ncovered airport workers; to repeal certain provisions of the labor law\nrelating thereto; and to repeal section 14 of a chapter of the laws of 2020\namending the labor law relating to enacting the \"healthy terminals act\", as\nproposed in legislative bills numbers S.6266-D and A.8142-E relating thereto",
    "basePrintNo": "S4001",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4001-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Rpld §230 subs 1-a, 15, 16 & 17, §231-a, §234 sub 1 ¶(e-1), amd Lab L, generally; rpld §14, Chap of 2020 (as proposed in S.6266-D & A.8142-E)",
    "lawSection": "Labor Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4001\n\nSPONSOR: BIAGGI\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the labor law in relation to removing certain provisions\nrelating to covered airport workers; and in relation to minimum wage\nrates for covered airport workers; to repeal certain provisions of the\nlabor law relating thereto; and to repeal section 14 of a chapter of the\nlaws of 2020 amending the labor law relating to enacting the \"healthy\nterminals act\", as proposed in legislative bills numbers S.6266-D and\nA.8142-E relating thereto\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to amend Chapter 387 of the Laws of 2020,\nestablishing the Healthy Terminals Act, in order to effectuate the\nintent of the law.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill would establish the legislative findings and\nintent.\n\nSections 2 through 15 of the bill would repeal various provisions of the\nLabor Law as added and/or amended by Chapter 387 of the Laws of 2020.\n\nSection 16 of the bill would establish a new Article 19-D of the Labor\nLaw to set forth the applicable standard rate for covered airport work-\ners at John F. Kennedy International Airport and LaGuardia Airport.\nSpecifically, this section would:\n\n*Define \"covered airport workers\" as any person employed to perform\ncleaning and related services, security related services, in terminal\nand passenger handling services, airline catering, or airport lounge\nservices and who work an average of at least 30 hours per week;\n\n*Require covered airport employers to compensate all covered airport\nworkers at a rate that is no less than the \"applicable standard rate\",\nwhich shall be a combination of:\n\n*The applicable minimum wage rate for such workers; and\n\n*The standard benefits supplement rate, by means of providing at least\n$4.54 per hour toward the cost of minimum essential coverage under an\nemployer. sponsored health insurance plan;\n\n*Require covered airport employers to provide covered airport workers\nwho are employed on December 30, 2020 with the ability to begin or\nchange enrollment in an eligible employer-sponsored health insurance\nplan for coverage beginning on July 1, 2021;\n\n*Require employers to provide covered airport workers who are employed\non or after January 1, 2023 with the ability to begin or change enroll-\nment in an eligible employer-sponsored health insurance plan for cover-\nage beginning no later than thirty days after becoming a covered airport\nworker;\n\n\n*Require each covered airport employer to submit a sworn statement to\nthe Commissioner of Labor by March 31, 2021 affirming that the number of\ncovered airport workers it employs between July 1, 2021 and December 30,\n2022 is equal to at least 80% of the total number of covered airport\nworkers employed on December 30, 2020;\n\n*Require each covered airport employer to submit a sworn statement to\nthe Commissioner of Labor by January 1, 2023 affirming that the propor-\ntion of covered airport workers it employs to work an average of at\nleast 30 hours per week, by classification, is the same proportion\ncompared to the 2019 workforce;\n\n*Require every covered airport employer to establish, maintain, and\npreserve payroll records for not less than six years;\n\n*Authorize the Commissioner of Labor to investigate the compensation of\ncovered airport workers in New York State and any violations thereof,\nincluding the examination of payroll records;\n\n*Establish civil penalties to be assessed against covered airport\nemployers for violations of such article, including:\n\n*Up to $10,000 for the first violation within six years;\n\n*Up to $20,000 for a second violation within six years; and\n\n*Up to $50,000 for a third and subsequent violation within six years;\n\n*Authorize the Commissioner of Labor to direct the payment of the stand-\nard benefits supplement rate to all employees, regardless of the number\nof hours worked, if it is determined that an employer withheld hours of\nwork for the purpose of reducing its obligations under such article; *\nAuthorize the Commissioner of Labor to bring any legal action necessary,\nincluding administrative action, on behalf of an employee paid less than\nthe applicable standard rate; * Authorize the Commissioner of Labor to\npromulgate regulations to carry out the purposes of such article; and *\nEstablish a savings clause in the event that any provision of such arti-\ncle is adjudged to be invalid or preempted by federal law.\n\nSection 17 of the bill would repeal the original savings clause of Chap-\nter 387 of the Laws of 2020.\n\nSection 18 of the bill would establish the effective date.\n\n \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN ORIGINAL AND AMENDED VERSION (IF APPLICABLE):\n\nThe amended version makes a technical change to the definition of\n\"covered airport location\" to include locations used to perform airline\ncatering work.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nA stable, trained, and experienced workforce is essential to the safe\nand secure operation of John F. Kennedy International Airport and\nLaGuardia Airport, which are some of the most heavily trafficked trans-\nportation centers in the world. From experience, we have seen that\nestablishing higher standards for wages and benefits is one of the best\nways to develop this type of strong and experienced workforce by\nattracting and motivating workers, increasing productivity, and reducing\nturnover.\n\nOver the past year, the COVID-19 pandemic has also highlighted the\nimportant roles played by airport workers on a daily basis, working\ntirelessly on the front lines at the intersection of public safety and\npublic health. Protecting the health and welfare of these individuals\ngoes hand in hand with keeping our airports ready, safe, and secure.\nHowever, employer-sponsored healthcare at these sites is often unafford-\nable, leaving many workers to rely on taxpayer-funded programs or else\ngo without healthcare entirely.\n\nFor these reasons, this bill would require employers to pay an hourly\nrate of at least $4.54 towards the cost of a qualified health insurance\nplan for covered airport workers at John F. Kennedy International\nAirport and LaGuardia Airport. It would also recognize the minimum wage\nstructure previously established for such workers by the Port Authority\nof New York and New Jersey. To ensure that employers do not withhold\nhours of work from covered airport workers in an attempt to skirt the\nlaw, this legislation would also require covered airport employers to\nmaintain certain workforce levels going forward. It would also empower\nthe Commissioner of Labor to require that an employer pay the hourly\nsupplement rate to all employees, regardless of the number of hours\nworked, if it is determined that such employer withheld hours of work to\nemployees for the purpose of reducing the employer's obligations.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act would take effect on the same date and in the same manner as a\nchapter of the laws of 2020 amending the labor law relating to enacting\nthe \"healthy terminals act\", as proposed in legislative bills numbers\n8.6266-D and A.8142-E, takes effect; provided, however that the commis-\nsioner of labor shall begin no investigation of compliance with this act\nbefore July 1, 2021 and no order issued pursuant to this act shall\ninclude any time period before July 1, 2021. Effective immediately, the\ncommissioner of labor may request documents or the preservation of docu-\nments relative to payroll and employee health plans for purposes of\ndetermining appropriate standards for full-time or part-time work.\n",
